Here is the Java code for Data Preprocessing and Association Rule Mining using Apriori in WEKA. It covers the Discretize, Resample, Remove, Normalize, and Apriori operations.

## 1. Data preprocessing using Discretize filter

This program converts numeric attributes into nominal intervals, which can then be used for association rule mining.

java
```

import weka.core.Instances;
import weka.core.converters.ConverterUtils.DataSource;
import weka.filters.Filter;
import weka.filters.unsupervised.attribute.Discretize;

public class DiscretizeExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        // Load Iris dataset
        DataSource source =
            new DataSource("data/iris.arff");
        Instances data = source.getDataSet();

        // Create Discretize filter
        Discretize filter = new Discretize();

        // Use 3 bins for numeric attributes
        filter.setBins(3);
        filter.setInputFormat(data);

        // Apply preprocessing
        Instances newData =
            Filter.useFilter(data, filter);

        // Display results
        System.out.println("Original Dataset:");
        System.out.println(data);

        System.out.println("Discretized Dataset:");
        System.out.println(newData);

        System.out.println("Preprocessing completed.");
    }
}
```

Output: Numeric attributes such as sepal length and petal width are converted into nominal intervals. The class attribute is retained.

## 2. Association rule mining using Apriori

This program loads the nominal weather dataset and generates association rules.

java
```

import weka.core.Instances;
import weka.core.converters.ConverterUtils.DataSource;
import weka.associations.Apriori;

public class AprioriExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        // Load nominal weather dataset
        DataSource source =
            new DataSource("data/weather.nominal.arff");
        Instances data = source.getDataSet();

        // Create Apriori model
        Apriori apriori = new Apriori();

        // Set minimum support and confidence
        apriori.setLowerBoundMinSupport(0.1);
        apriori.setMinMetric(0.8);

        // Set maximum number of rules
        apriori.setNumRules(10);

        // Build association rules
        apriori.buildAssociations(data);

        // Display generated rules
        System.out.println("Association Rules:");
        System.out.println(apriori);
    }
}
```

Output: The program displays frequent itemsets and association rules, with their support and confidence information.

- Support: Frequency of an itemset in the dataset.
- Confidence: How often the consequent occurs when the antecedent occurs.
- Example rule: `outlook=overcast → play=yes`

The actual rules depend on the dataset and the selected parameters.

## 3. Combined program: Discretization + Apriori

Use this program for numeric datasets such as `iris.arff` and `glass.arff`.

java
```

import weka.core.Instances;
import weka.core.converters.ConverterUtils.DataSource;
import weka.filters.Filter;
import weka.filters.unsupervised.attribute.Discretize;
import weka.associations.Apriori;

public class DiscretizeApriori {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        // Load dataset
        DataSource source =
            new DataSource("data/iris.arff");
        Instances data = source.getDataSet();

        // Discretize numeric attributes
        Discretize discretize = new Discretize();
        discretize.setBins(3);
        discretize.setInputFormat(data);

        Instances nominalData =
            Filter.useFilter(data, discretize);

        // Apply Apriori
        Apriori apriori = new Apriori();
        apriori.setLowerBoundMinSupport(0.1);
        apriori.setMinMetric(0.8);
        apriori.setNumRules(10);

        apriori.buildAssociations(nominalData);

        // Print results
        System.out.println("Discretized data:");
        System.out.println(nominalData);

        System.out.println("Apriori Association Rules:");
        System.out.println(apriori);
    }
}
```

## 4. Other preprocessing filters

### A. Resample filter

Creates a random sample of the dataset.

java
```

import weka.core.Instances;
import weka.core.converters.ConverterUtils.DataSource;
import weka.filters.Filter;
import weka.filters.unsupervised.instance.Resample;

public class ResampleExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Instances data = new DataSource(
            "data/iris.arff").getDataSet();

        Resample filter = new Resample();
        filter.setSampleSizePercent(50);
        filter.setNoReplacement(true);
        filter.setRandomSeed(1);
        filter.setInputFormat(data);

        Instances sampled =
            Filter.useFilter(data, filter);

        System.out.println("Original instances: "
            + data.numInstances());
        System.out.println("Sampled instances: "
            + sampled.numInstances());
    }
}
```

### B. Remove filter

Removes a specified attribute. For example, remove the fifth attribute.

java
```

import weka.core.Instances;
import weka.core.converters.ConverterUtils.DataSource;
import weka.filters.Filter;
import weka.filters.unsupervised.attribute.Remove;

public class RemoveExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Instances data = new DataSource(
            "data/iris.arff").getDataSet();

        Remove filter = new Remove();
        filter.setAttributeIndices("5");
        filter.setInputFormat(data);

        Instances result =
            Filter.useFilter(data, filter);

        System.out.println(result);
    }
}
```

### C. Normalize filter

Scales numeric attribute values to the range 0–1.

java
```

import weka.core.Instances;
import weka.core.converters.ConverterUtils.DataSource;
import weka.filters.Filter;
import weka.filters.unsupervised.attribute.Normalize;

public class NormalizeExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Instances data = new DataSource(
            "data/iris.arff").getDataSet();

        Normalize filter = new Normalize();
        filter.setInputFormat(data);

        Instances normalized =
            Filter.useFilter(data, filter);

        System.out.println(normalized);
    }
}
```

